            <div xml:id="d538">

                <pb facs="0752" n="672" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg672"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">641.0031/65: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-06-26T18:00:00+01:00">June 26, 1936—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 26—2:10 p.m]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>326. At his request I had a further conversation with Chamberlain today, report
                    of which goes forward by next pouch.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d538-f1"/> After
                    emphasizing rapid rate of political deterioration in Europe and the opening
                    caused by Germany’s clearing arrangements, et cetera, he referred to this
                    country’s pending trade agreement negotiations and frankly expressed the fear
                    lest he should be branded with “inconsistency” should they make the contemplated
                    public policy declaration while these trade agreements were in process of
                    negotiation. I felt his attitude as well as Runciman’s reported in my 323, of
                    June 25, 5 p.m., was based on Cabinet consultation and decision to play for
                    time.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atherton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
